{:edb/db-before-t 6135 :edb/db-after-t 6136 :edb/tx-data [[13194139539448 50 #inst "2026-10-24T01:55:50.914Z" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186508983 1074 "To find an amount that's $15%$ *less than* $$85,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find $85%$ of $$85,$ we multiply $$85$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot $85=$72.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the chair is $$72.25.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186508983 1074 "To find an amount that's $15\\%$ *less than* $\\$85,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find $85\\%$ of $\\$85,$ we multiply $\\$85$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot $85=$72.25\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sale price of the chair is $\\$72.25.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186508985 1071 "![](images/31/31df5fe79f2aa8715321b6500474683a01732788e5fafc3300dfffc83c266893.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $3$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186508985 1071 "![](images/31/31df5fe79f2aa8715321b6500474683a01732788e5fafc3300dfffc83c266893.png)\n\nA box whose width $x$ is $3$ times its depth $z$ has a manufacturing cost that depends on two materials. The material for the top and bottom costs $\\$5$ per square foot, and the material for the lateral faces costs $\\$2$ per square foot. The box must have a volume of $50\\,{ft}^{3}.$\n\nWhat is the value of $z$ for the box that has the smallest production cost?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186508992 1071 "What number is $15%$ less than $100$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186508992 1071 "What number is $15\\%$ less than $100$?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186508992 1074 "To find a number $15%$ *less than* $100,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%-15%=85%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85%$ of $100,$ we multiply $100$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 100=85\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186508992 1074 "To find a number $15\\%$ *less than* $100,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\%-15\\%=85\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n85\\%=\\frac{85}{100}=0.85\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $85\\%$ of $100,$ we multiply $100$ by $0.85:$\n\n$$\n0.85\\cdot 100=85\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186508994 1071 "Joel invested $$11\\,280.$ The investment performed poorly, losing one-eighth of its value every month.\n\nThe function $p(t)$ models the value of the investment after $t$ months and is given by\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186508994 1071 "Joel invested $\\$11\\,280.$ The investment performed poorly, losing one-eighth of its value every month.\n\nThe function $p(t)$ models the value of the investment after $t$ months and is given by\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\text{___})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat number is missing?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186508994 1074 "The initial investment was $$11\\,280$, and this decreased by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month.\n\nIf the investment value is reduced by $\\frac{1}{8},$ then $\\frac{7}{8}$ of its value remained every month. So the decay factor is $\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\np=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=11\\,280$ is the initial investment.\n\n- $b=\\frac{7}{8}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p(t)$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{7}{8}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186508994 1074 "The initial investment was $\\$11\\,280$, and this decreased by $\\frac{1}{8}$ every month.\n\nIf the investment value is reduced by $\\frac{1}{8},$ then $\\frac{7}{8}$ of its value remained every month. So the decay factor is $\\frac{7}{8}.$\n\nThe formula for exponential decay (in terms of a decay factor $b$) is\n\n$$\np=a{b}^{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- $a=11\\,280$ is the initial investment.\n\n- $b=\\frac{7}{8}$ is the decay factor.\n\n- $t=$ the time, in months.\n\nTherefore, the correct expression for $p(t)$ is\n\n$$\np(t)=11\\,280\\cdot {(\\frac{7}{8})}^{t}.\n$$\n\nSo the missing number is $\\frac{7}{8}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186510379 1071 "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4.6$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=73$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=33$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=26,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186510379 1071 "Consider a sample of size ${n}_{1}=82$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{1}=4.6$ and a sample of size ${n}_{2}=73$ from a population with standard deviation ${\\sigma }_{2}=5.2.$ Given that the samples are independent and the sample means are ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}=33$ and  ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=26,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05,$ where $Z∼N(0,1).$*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186510379 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=33-26=7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4.6}^{2}}{82}+\\frac{{5.2}^{2}}{73}}≈1.304\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 7\\pm 1.304\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186510379 1074 "Notice that\n\n- the samples are independent,\n\n- both sample sizes are \"sufficiently large,\" i.e., $\\ge 30,$ and\n\n- both population variances are known.\n\nAs a result, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the difference ${\\mu }_{1}-{\\mu }_{2}$ between the corresponding population means can be written as\n\n$$\n({\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2})\\pm E\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $$\n\\displaystyle E={z}_{\\alpha /2}\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}}{{n}_{1}}+\\frac{{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}}{{n}_{2}}}\n$$ is the margin of error,\n\n- ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}$ and ${\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}$ are the sample means, ${\\mu }_{1}$ and ${\\mu }_{2}$ are the population means, ${\\sigma }_{1}^{2}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}^{2}$ are the population variances,  ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are the sample sizes,\n\n- $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nNow, to construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{―}{x}}_{1}-{\\overset{―}{x}}_{2}=33-26=7\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90\\%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.645)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.05}=1.645.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\nE=1.645\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{4.6}^{2}}{82}+\\frac{{5.2}^{2}}{73}}≈1.304\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Construct the $90\\%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & 7\\pm 1.304\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186510381 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=18-{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(18-{x}^{2})=18x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=18-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 18-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =6 \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,\\sqrt{6})$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ | $(\\sqrt{6},3\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ |  | $4$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $x=\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(\\sqrt{6}) & =18(\\sqrt{6})-{(\\sqrt{6})}^{3} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186510381 1074 "Let $x$ and $y$ be the width and height of the rectangle, respectively. Given a width $x,$ the height $y$ of the rectangle rises up to the curve $y=18-{x}^{2}.$\n\nWe want to maximize the area of the rectangle, which is given by\n\n$$\nA=xy=x(18-{x}^{2})=18x-{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nLet's compute the critical points. Taking the derivative of the area, we get\n\n$$\n{A}^{′}(x)=18-3{x}^{2},\n$$\n\nand solving ${A}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 18-3{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =6 \\\\ x & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $x=\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $A(x).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,\\sqrt{6})$ | $\\sqrt{6}$ | $(\\sqrt{6},3\\sqrt{2})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $1$ | $$ | $4$ |\n| ${A}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $A$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $x=\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum, and the area of the resulting rectangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest area ${A}_{\\text{max}}$ that a rectangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{\\text{max}}=A(\\sqrt{6}) & =18(\\sqrt{6})-{(\\sqrt{6})}^{3} \\\\  & =18\\sqrt{6}-6\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =12\\sqrt{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186510392 1074 "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP(l)=6\\sqrt{3}+l+\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $6\\sqrt{3},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<6\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(-2l)}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =108-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =54 \\\\ l & =3\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=3\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,3\\sqrt{6})$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $(3\\sqrt{6},6\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=3\\sqrt{6}$ gives the maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{max}}=P(3\\sqrt{6}) & =6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{108-54} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{54} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}+6\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186510392 1074 "Let's call $l$ the length of one of the legs. By the Pythagorean theorem, the length of the other leg is $\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}.$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter is given by\n\n$$\nP(l)=6\\sqrt{3}+l+\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSince $l$ is the length of a leg of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is $6\\sqrt{3},$ the permitted range of $l$ is $0<l<6\\sqrt{3}.$\n\nWe want to maximize $P(l),$ so we need to compute the critical points. Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{(-2l)}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} \\\\  & =1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(l)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(l) & =0 \\\\ 1-\\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{l}{\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}}} & =1 \\\\ l & =\\sqrt{108-{l}^{2}} \\\\ {l}^{2} & =108-{l}^{2} \\\\ 2{l}^{2} & =108 \\\\ {l}^{2} & =54 \\\\ l & =3\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll that's left now is to confirm that $l=3\\sqrt{6}$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(l).$ To do this, we use the first derivative test. We write up a table of values:\n\n| Interval | $(0,3\\sqrt{6})$ | $3\\sqrt{6}$ | $(3\\sqrt{6},6\\sqrt{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ | $$ | $9$ |\n| ${P}^{′}$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $P$ | $↗$ | $\\text{max}$ | $↘$ |\n\nSo, $l=3\\sqrt{6}$ gives the maximum, and the perimeter of the resulting triangle will be the largest possible.\n\nTherefore, the greatest perimeter ${P}_{\\text{max}}$ that a triangle can have is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{\\text{max}}=P(3\\sqrt{6}) & =6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{108-54} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}+3\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{54} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}+6\\sqrt{6} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}(1+\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186510397 1071 "The scores of girls and 