11 false] [17592186511522 1071 "A coffee shop can produce up to $500$ cups of coffee per day. When the price per cup of coffee is $\\$3,$ the coffee shop sells all of them, and, for each dollar increase in the price, $50$ cups remain unsold. If the cost for producing a cup of coffee is $\\$0.40,$ what price per cup should be set to obtain the maximum profit?" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186511522 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =\\underbrace{(5-x)}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{(3+2x)}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $$6.7.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186511522 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that if there are $x$ hundreds of *unsold* cups of coffee, the coffee shop sells $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, and the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. So, the total revenue (in hundreds of dollars) is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =\\underbrace{(5-x)}_{\\text{sold cups of coffee}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{(3+2x)}_{\\text{price per cup}} \\\\  & =15+10x-3x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =15+7x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, for selling $(5-x)$ hundreds of cups of coffee, the coffee shop has a cost (in hundreds of dollars) of \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C(x) & =0.4(5-x) \\\\  & =2-0.4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\ P(x) & =(15+7x-2{x}^{2})-(2-0.4x) \\\\  & =13+7.4x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $0\\le x\\le 5.$ \n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $0<x<5.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=7.4-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 7.4-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =7.4 \\\\ x & =1.85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=1.85$ lies in the interval $0<x<5.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=1.85$ does indeed give a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=1.85$ is indeed a local maximum of $P(x).$ And since $P(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=1.85$ is a global maximum of $P(x).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that the coffee shop will have a maximum profit level when there are $185$ unsold cups of coffee.  \n\nWe know that the price per cup is $(3+2x)$ dollars. If there are $1.85$ hundreds of unsold cups of coffee, then the price per cup is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+2(1.85) & =$6.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coffee shop should sell each cup of coffee for $\\$6.7.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186511566 1071 "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is $$$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186511566 1071 "A candle company spends $C(x)=120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}$ dollars to produce $x$ candles per week. The company sells each candle for $\\$120.$ Given that the company can produce between $1$ and $6$ candles per week, what is the maximum weekly profit that the company can make?\n\nThe maximum weekly profit is \\$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186511566 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $$\\,1296.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186511566 1074 "To solve this problem, we apply the candidates test.\n\nFirst, note that for selling $x$ candles, the company has a revenue (income) of $R(x)=120\\cdot x=120x$ dollars. \n\nTherefore, the profit function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x) & =R(x)-C(x) \\\\  & =120x-(120x-24{x}^{2}+16{x}^{3}-3{x}^{4}) \\\\  & =24{x}^{2}-16{x}^{3}+3{x}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe note that the permitted domain is $1\\le x\\le 6.$ Therefore, $x=1$ and $x=6$ are candidates for the global maxima of $P(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $P(x)$ for $1<x<6.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(x)=48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nand solving ${P}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 48x-48{x}^{2}+12{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ 12x(4-4x+{x}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 12x(x-2)^{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the critical points are $x=0$ and $x=2.$ However, the value $x=0$ is not in the permitted domain, so the only possible new candidate is $x=2.$\n\nLet's check if $x=2$ gives a maximum value of $P(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(x)=48-96x+36{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating at $x=2,$ we get\n\n$$\n{P}^{″}(2)=48-192+144=0,\n$$\n\nwhich does not confirm that $x=2$ is a maximum. Therefore, we must include $x=2$ as a candidate.\n\nOur candidates are $x=1,$ $x=2,$ and $x=6.$ Computing $P(x)$ at each of these values, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(1) & =24(1)^{2}-16(1)^{3}+3(1)^{4}=11 \\\\ P(2) & =24(2)^{2}-16(2)^{3}+3(2)^{4}=32 \\\\ P(6) & =24(6)^{2}-16(6)^{3}+3(6)^{4}=1296\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the maximum weekly profit that the company can make is $\\$\\,1296.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186511575 1071 "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $$700$ and a variable cost of $$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186511575 1071 "A theme park sells ride passes each day. The price of each ride pass is $\\$15.$ The cost of operating the park includes a fixed daily cost of $\\$700$ and a variable cost of $\\$2$ per ride pass sold. If the park can sell at most $500$ ride passes per day, what is the number of ride passes that maximizes the park's profit?\n\nThe number of ride passes that maximizes the daily profit is\n{{field-1}}." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186512871 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=96$ from a population where $72%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the pr