r the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186512932 1071 "Suppose we take a random sample of size $n=88$ from a population where $44\\%$ of the population has a particular characteristic. Find a normal approximation for the probability that more than $52\\%$ of the sample has the characteristic. You may assume that the population size is significantly larger than the sample size.\n\n*Hint: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186512938 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to choose $5$ people out of a total of $12,$ and $n$ is the number of picks where $2$ are boys and $3$ are girls.\n\nSince the order in which the players are chosen does not matter, we use combinations.\n\nFirst, we compute $N.$ The number of ways to pick $5$ people from a total of $12$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N={}_{12}{C}_{5} & =\\frac{12!}{5!\\cdot 7!}=792.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute $n.$ The computation of $n$ can be divided into two steps: \n\n- the number of ways to choose $2$ (out of $7$) boys is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{7}{C}_{2}=\\frac{7!}{2!\\cdot 5!}=21\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the number of ways to choose $3$ (out of $5$) girls is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{}_{5}{C}_{3} & =\\frac{5!}{3!\\cdot 2!}=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the results from the above steps, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}n={}_{7}{C}_{2}\\cdot {}_{5}{C}_{3}=21\\cdot 10=210.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{210}{792}≈0.27=27%.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186512938 1074 "Let $A$ represent the event described in the question. Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =\\frac{n}{N},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $N$ is the total number of ways to choose $5$ people out of a total of $12,$ an