pulation mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515537 1071 "![](images/28/28f56656e191e342a4815ba74bdd29cba5247fba7d8ab0feca8ffbe0b785ab8c.png)\n\nConsider a sample of size $n=120$  from a population with standard deviation $\\sigma =10.$ Given that the mean of the sample is $\\overset{―}{x}=3,$ find a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the distribution.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025,$ where $Z∼N(0,1),$ as shown in the diagram above.*" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186515537 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{120}} \\\\ 3\\pm 1.789\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515537 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population. However, the sample size $n=120\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*. Therefore, according to the central limit theorem, we may use the following approximation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{\\sigma /\\sqrt{n}}∼N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${\\sigma }^{2}$ is the population variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sigma }{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(Z>{z}_{\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2}$ and $Z∼N(0,1).$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(Z>1.96)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{z}_{\\alpha /2}={z}_{0.025}=1.96.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\pm 1.96\\cdot \\frac{10}{\\sqrt{120}} \\\\ 3\\pm 1.789\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186515539 1071 "A total of $200$ colored table tennis balls are distributed among three boxes, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $20$ green balls, while Box $B$ contains $30$ green balls. If $45%$ of all the balls are green, what is the probability that a randomly selected ball is a green ball from Box $C?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515539 1071 "A total of $200$ colored table tennis balls are distributed among three boxes, $A,$ $B,$ and $C.$ Box $A$ contains $20$ green balls, while Box $B$ contains $30$ green balls. If $45\\%$ of all the balls are green, what is the probability that a randomly selected ball is a green ball from Box $C?$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186515539 1074 "Let $G$ be the event that the selected ball is green, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(G\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap A),$ we take the number of green balls in box $A$ $(20)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap A)=\\frac{20}{200}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap B),$ we take the number of green balls in box $B$ $(30)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap B)=\\frac{30}{200}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(G)=45%=\\frac{90}{200}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{90}{200} & =\\frac{20}{200}+\\frac{30}{200}+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{90}{200}-\\frac{20}{200}-\\frac{30}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =20%\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515539 1074 "Let $G$ be the event that the selected ball is green, and\n\n- let $A$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $A$,\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $B$,\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the selected ball is from box $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nRecall that the law of total probability states that\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C).\n$$\n\nWe wish to find $P(G\\cap C):$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap A),$ we take the number of green balls in box $A$ $(20)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap A)=\\frac{20}{200}.\n$$\n\n- To calculate $P(G\\cap B),$ we take the number of green balls in box $B$ $(30)$ and divide by the total number of balls $(200).$ So, we have \n\n$$\nP(G\\cap B)=\\frac{30}{200}.\n$$\n\n- Also, we're given that\n\n$$\nP(G)=45\\%=\\frac{90}{200}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G\\cap C),$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G\\cap A)+P(G\\cap B)+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ \\frac{90}{200} & =\\frac{20}{200}+\\frac{30}{200}+P(G\\cap C) \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{90}{200}-\\frac{20}{200}-\\frac{30}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =\\frac{40}{200} \\\\ P(G\\cap C) & =20\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186515540 1071 "$$\n\nFind the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡{x}^{2}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515540 1071 "Find the derivative of $f(x)=-\\operatorname{csch}⁡{x}^{2}.$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186515541 1071 "In a particular park, there are three forests. Forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park area, and  $30%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $B$ occupies $25%$ of the park area, and $20%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $C$ occupies the remaining park area, and $6%$ of its plants are poisonous. What is the probability that a randomly selected plant from this park is poisonous? Assume that the plants are evenly distributed throughout the park." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515541 1071 "In a particular park, there are three forests. Forest $A$ occupies $40\\%$ of the park area, and  $30\\%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $B$ occupies $25\\%$ of the park area, and $20\\%$ of its plants are poisonous. Forest $C$ occupies the remaining park area, and $6\\%$ of its plants are poisonous. What is the probability that a randomly selected plant from this park is poisonous? Assume that the plants are evenly distributed throughout the park." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186515541 1074 "Let $V$ be the event that the plant is poisonous, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the plant is from forest $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the plant is from forest $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the plant is from forest $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap A)+P(V\\cap B)+P(V\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each piece of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since forest $A$ occupies $40%$ of the park area and forest $B$ occupies $25%$, then forest $C$ occupies the remaining\n\n$$\n100%-(40%+25%)=35%.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.4,\\,P(B)=0.25,\\,P(C)=0.35.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the plants in forest $A,$ $20%$ of the plants in forest $B$, and $6%$ of the plants in forrest $C$ are poisonous, we have\n\n$$\nP(V|A)=0.3,\\,P(V|B)=0.2,\\,P(V|C)=0.06.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected plant in that park is poisonous is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C) \\\\  & =(0.3)(0.4)+(0.2)(0.25)+(0.06)(0.35) \\\\  & =0.12+0.05+0.021 \\\\  & =0.191.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186515541 1074 "Let $V$ be the event that the plant is poisonous, and \n\n- let $A$ be the event that the plant is from forest $A,$\n\n- let $B$ be the event that the plant is from forest $B,$\n\n- let $C$ be the event that the plant is from forest $C.$\n\nThen the disjoint union $A⊔B⊔C$ covers the entire sample space.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected plant is poisonous, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap A)+P(V\\cap B)+P(V\\cap C) \\\\  & =P(V|A)P(A)+P(V|B)P(B)+P(V|C)P(C)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's go thr