rom their home to their workplace. The sample mean distance was $8.52\\,\\text{mi}$ with standard deviation $2.40\\,\\text{mi}.$ Find a $99\\%$ confidence interval for the mean distance from home to work  $\\mu $ for all working adults in this state.   \n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{k}$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores ${t}_{k,p}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{k,p})=p$ for some particular values of $k$ and $p.$*\n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186521479 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8.52\\,\\text{mi},\\,n=400,\\,s=2.40\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{399,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{399,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=400-1=399$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/82/829fa68b18545ff59ffa702fe4567997d635089edf4e90d713b1c8d817c7959f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{399,0.005}=2.588:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $2.588$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.52 & \\pm 2.588\\cdot \\frac{2.40}{\\sqrt{400}}\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ 8.52 & \\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186521479 1074 "We do not know the distribution of the population, nor do we know the population variance ${\\sigma }^{2}.$ However, the sample size $n=400\\ge 30$ is *sufficiently large*.  \n\nIn cases like this, the random variable\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\overset{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\ncan be approximated by the student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where \n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=8.52\\,\\text{mi},\\,n=400,\\,s=2.40\\,\\text{mi}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $99\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{399,0.005}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{399,0.005})=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=400-1=399$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/82/829fa68b18545ff59ffa702fe4567997d635089edf4e90d713b1c8d817c7959f.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{399,0.005}=2.588:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $398$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n| $399$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $2.588$ |\n| $400$ | $\\,1.284\\,$ | $\\,1.649\\,$ | $\\,1.966\\,$ | $\\,2.336\\,$ | $\\,2.588\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, the $99\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.52 & \\pm 2.588\\cdot \\frac{2.40}{\\sqrt{400}}\\,\\text{mi} \\\\ 8.52 & \\pm 0.311\\,\\text{mi}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186521488 1071 "Ethan and Lucas are swimmers. In the previous season, $75%$ of Ethan’s races were freestyle, while the decimal proportion of Lucas’s races that were freestyle was $0.4.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Ethan's races were freestyle.\n\nII. More than half of Lucas's races were freestyle.\n\nIII. Ethan had a greater percentage of freestyle races compared to Lucas." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186521488 1071 "Ethan and Lucas are swimmers. In the previous season, $75\\%$ of Ethan’s races were freestyle, while the decimal proportion of Lucas’s races that were freestyle was $0.4.$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. Less than half of Ethan's races were freestyle.\n\nII. M