set{―}{X}-\\mu }{S/\\sqrt{n}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution ${T}_{n-1}$ with $n-1$ degrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the sample mean,\n\n- $\\mu $ is the population mean,\n\n- ${S}^{2}$ is the sample variance, and\n\n- $n$ is the sample size.\n\nGiven a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ of the original distribution is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}\\pm {t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\frac{s}{\\sqrt{n}},\n$$\n\nwhere $P(T>{t}_{n-1,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ and $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1$ degrees of freedom.\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{x}=13.1\\,\\text{g/dl},\\,n=16,\\,s=1.8\\,\\text{g/dl}.\n$$\n\nWe are interested in finding a $95\\%$ confidence interval. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe also need to find the $t$-score value ${t}_{15,0.025}$ such that $P(T>{t}_{15,0.025})=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-1=16-1=15$ degrees of freedom.\n\n![](images/03/03b3b3aec86ef22237d0013b3496993ae4cdda192540fc1c29f01ad608fcb810.png)\n\nFrom the percentage points table of the $t$-distribution, we obtain that ${t}_{15,0.025}=2.131:$ \n\n| $k∖p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $15$ | $1.341$ | $1.753$ | $2.131$ | $2.602$ | $2.947$ |\n| $16$ | $1.337$ | $1.746$ | $2.120$ | $2.583$ | $2.921$ |\n| $17$ | $1.333$ | $1.740$ | $2.110$ | $2.576$ | $2.898$ |\n\nTherefore, a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population mean $\\mu $ is the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13.1 & \\pm 2.131\\cdot \\frac{1.8}{\\sqrt{16}}\\,\\text{g/dl} \\\\ 13.1 & \\pm 0.959\\,\\text{g/dl}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186522700 1071 "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32%$ of all females and $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51%$ of the population are female. If a person who eats after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186522700 1071 "A survey of adult citizens of a particular city showed that $32\\%$ of all females and $5\\%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m. $51\\%$ of the population are female. If a person who eats after $6$ p.m. is selected at random, what is the probability of them being male? Express your answer as a percentage to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186522700 1074 "Let $E$ be the event that a randomly selected person eats after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|E),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|E)=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E)}=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32%$ of all females and  $5%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(E|M)=1-0.05=0.95,\\,P(E|{M}^{′})=1-0.32=0.68.\n$$\n\n- Since $51%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|E) & =\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.49)}{(0.95)(0.49)+(0.68)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈57%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186522700 1074 "Let $E$ be the event that a randomly selected person eats after $6$ p.m., and let $M$ be the event that they are male. Then, we require $P(M|E),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(M|E)=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E)}=\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since $32\\%$ of all females and  $5\\%$ of all males don't eat after $6$ p.m, we have\n\n$$\nP(E|M)=1-0.05=0.95,\\,P(E|{M}^{′})=1-0.32=0.68.\n$$\n\n- Since $51\\%$ of the population are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(M)=1-0.51=0.49,\\,P({M}^{′})=0.51.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(M|E) & =\\frac{P(E|M)P(M)}{P(E|M)P(M)+P(E|{M}^{′})P({M}^{′})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.95)(0.49)}{(0.95)(0.49)+(0.68)(0.51)} \\\\  & ≈57\\%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest percent." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186522727 1071 "On a particular industrial estate, the probability of smoke on a given day is $10%$ and the probability of a dangerous fire is $1.5%.$ It is known that $90%$ of dangerous fires create smoke. What is the probability of a dangerous fire when there is smoke? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186522727 1071 "On a particular industrial estate, the probability of smoke on a given day is $10\\%$ and the probability of a dangerous fire is $1.5\\%.$ It is known that $90\\%$ of dangerous fires create smoke. What is the probability of a dangerous fire when there is smoke? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186522727 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that there is smoke, and let $F$ be the event that there is a dangerous fire. Then, we require $P(F|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|S)=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\n- $P(S)=0.1,$ since the probability of smoke is $10%.$\n\n- $P(F)=0.015,$ since the probability of dangerous fire is $1.5%.$\n\n- $P(S|F)=0.9,$ since  $90%$ of dangerous fires make smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|S) & =\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.9\\cdot 0.015}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈0.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186522727 1074 "Let $S$ be the event that there is smoke, and let $F$ be the event that there is a dangerous fire. Then, we require $P(F|S),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP(F|S)=\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)}\n$$\n\n- $P(S)=0.1,$ since the probability of smoke is $10\\%.$\n\n- $P(F)=0.015,$ since the probability of dangerous fire is $1.5\\%.$\n\n- $P(S|F)=0.9,$ since  $90\\%$ of dangerous fires make smoke.\n\nTherefore, using Bayes' theorem, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|S) & =\\frac{P(S|F)P(F)}{P(S)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.9\\cdot 0.015}{0.1} \\\\  & ≈0.14,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186522738 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $94%$ of those infected and $7%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $12%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected? Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 false] [17592186522738 1071 "A laboratory test for a particular disease is conducted on some patients. Of the tested patients, $94\\%$ of those infected and $7\\%$ of those not infected tested positive for the disease. Only $12\\%$ of the entire population is infected with the disease. What is the probability that a randomly chosen patient that tested negative is not infected? Round your answer to four decimal places." 13194139539448 17592186576411 true] [17592186522738 1074 "Let $D$ be the event that a randomly chosen patient is infected with the disease, and let $T$ be the event they tested positive. Then, we require $P({D}^{′}|{T}^{′}),$ which we can compute using Bayes' theorem.\n\n$$\nP({D}^{′}|{T}^{′})=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′})}=\\frac{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})}{P({T}^{′}|{D}^{′})P({D}^{′})+P({T}^{′}|D)P(D)}\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we have the following:\n\n- Since the probability of an infected person testing positive is $94%,$ and the probability of a non-infected person testing positive is $7%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP({